QUESTIONS=[
 r'Evaluate by substitution: (a) \(\int\sin^4x\cos x\,dx\); (b) \(\int\sqrt[3]{\sin^2x}\cos x\,dx\); (c) \(\int\frac{\sin x\ln(\cos x)}{\cos x}\,dx\); (d) \(\int e^x\sin(e^x)\,dx\); (e) \(\int\frac{\cot\sqrt x}{\sqrt x}\,dx\); (f) \(\int\frac{\sin x-\cos x}{\sin x+\cos x}\,dx\).',
 r'Use suitable substitutions and tables: (a) \(\int\frac{dx}{x^2+16}\); (b) \(\int\frac{\sin x}{\cos^2x+1}\,dx\); (c) \(\int\frac{dx}{\sqrt{e^{2x}-4}}\); (d) \(\int\frac{2x+5}{x^2+4x+5}\,dx\); (e) \(\int\frac{2+x}{\sqrt{4-2x-x^2}}\,dx\).',
 r'Evaluate using integration by parts: (a) \(\int x^2e^{2x}\,dx\); (b) \(\int x\cos x\,dx\); (c) \(\int e^{2x}\sin x\,dx\); (d) \(\int e^x\cos x\,dx\); (e) \(\int\sin^{-1}x\,dx\); (f) \(\int e^{3t}\sin(e^{3t})\,dt\).'
]
STEPS=[
 ['(a) Let u = sin x, du = cos x dx. Integrate u⁴: sin⁵x/5+C.', '(b) With u = sin x, the integrand becomes ∛(u²) du = |u|^(2/3) du. A real antiderivative is (3/5)u|u|^(2/3)+C, giving (3/5)sin x|sin x|^(2/3)+C, valid even at sine zeros.', '(c) The printed ln(cos x) requires cos x > 0. Set u = ln(cos x), du = −sin x dx/cos x. Integrate −u du: −[ln(cos x)]²/2+C on each original-domain interval.', '(d) Set u = eˣ, du = eˣdx. Integrate sin u: −cos(eˣ)+C.', '(e) Set u = √x, dx = 2u du, with x > 0. The integral becomes 2∫cot u du = 2ln|sin√x|+C, where sin√x ≠ 0.', '(f) Set u = sin x+cos x. Then du = (cos x−sin x)dx, the negative of the numerator. The result is −ln|sin x+cos x|+C, where sin x+cos x ≠ 0.', 'Differentiate each final expression on its original real domain. A logarithm-of-absolute-value extension in (c) would not change the source domain cos x > 0.'],
 ['(a) Put u = x/4. Then dx/(x²+16) = (1/4)du/(u²+1), giving (1/4)arctan(x/4)+C.', '(b) Put u = cos x, du = −sin x dx. Integrate −du/(1+u²): −arctan(cos x)+C.', '(c) The real denominator requires e²ˣ > 4, so x > ln 2. Put u = eˣ/2 > 1, dx = du/u.', 'Then the integral is (1/2)∫du/[u√(u²−1)] = (1/2)arcsec u+C = (1/2)arccos(2e⁻ˣ)+C on x > ln 2.', '(d) Complete the square: x²+4x+5 = (x+2)²+1. Split the numerator into (2x+4)+1.', 'The first integral is ln(x²+4x+5); the second is arctan(x+2). Sum: ln(x²+4x+5)+arctan(x+2)+C, for all real x.', '(e) Put u = x+1. Then the denominator is √(5−u²) and the numerator is u+1.', 'Split into ∫u/√(5−u²) du + ∫du/√(5−u²). The result is −√(4−2x−x²)+arcsin((x+1)/√5)+C, on −1−√5 < x < −1+√5.'],
 ['(a) Choose u = x²,dv = e²ˣdx, giving v = e²ˣ/2 and du = 2x dx. The first step is x²e²ˣ/2−∫xe²ˣdx.', 'Apply parts again to ∫xe²ˣdx: xe²ˣ/2−e²ˣ/4. Thus (a) = e²ˣ(x²/2−x/2+1/4)+C.', '(b) Choose u = x,dv = cos x dx. The answer is xsin x−∫sin x dx = xsin x+cos x+C.', '(c) Let I = ∫e²ˣsin x dx and J = ∫e²ˣcos x dx. Parts gives I = −e²ˣcos x+2J and J = e²ˣsin x−2I.', 'Substitute and collect: 5I = e²ˣ(2sin x−cos x). Thus (c) = e²ˣ(2sin x−cos x)/5+C.', '(d) Applying parts twice with exponent coefficient 1 gives eˣ(sin x+cos x)/2+C.', '(e) Use u = arcsin x,dv = dx. Then ∫arcsin x dx = xarcsin x−∫x/√(1−x²) dx = xarcsin x+√(1−x²)+C, for −1 < x < 1.', '(f) Substitution is more direct: u = e³ᵗ gives du = 3e³ᵗdt. Thus ∫e³ᵗsin(e³ᵗ)dt = −cos(e³ᵗ)/3+C. If demonstrating parts, take u = sin(e³ᵗ),dv = e³ᵗdt; the resulting integral returns to the same substitution.', 'Differentiate (f): (−1/3)(−sin(e³ᵗ))(3e³ᵗ) = e³ᵗsin(e³ᵗ), confirming the factor 1/3.']
]
ANSWERS=['(a) sin⁵x/5+C; (b) (3/5)sin x|sin x|^(2/3)+C; (c) −[ln(cos x)]²/2+C; (d) −cos(eˣ)+C; (e) 2ln|sin√x|+C; (f) −ln|sin x+cos x|+C.', '(a) arctan(x/4)/4+C; (b) −arctan(cos x)+C; (c) arccos(2e⁻ˣ)/2+C; (d) ln(x²+4x+5)+arctan(x+2)+C; (e) −√(4−2x−x²)+arcsin((x+1)/√5)+C.', '(a) e²ˣ(x²/2−x/2+1/4)+C; (b) xsin x+cos x+C; (c) e²ˣ(2sin x−cos x)/5+C; (d) eˣ(sin x+cos x)/2+C; (e) xarcsin x+√(1−x²)+C; (f) −cos(e³ᵗ)/3+C.']
PAGES=[page('085_page_2.jpg',162,[heading('Remember'),paragraph('Here’s very helpful mnemonic for an order of priority for which factor the derivative must be passed to.'),{'type':'table','caption':'ILATE','rows':[['I','Inverse, trigonometric'],['L','Logarithms'],['A','Algebraic'],['T','Trigonometric'],['E','Exponential']]},heading('Exercise 6.2')]+[paragraph(str(i+1)+'. '+q) for i,q in enumerate(QUESTIONS)]+[heading('History'),paragraph('Archimedes was a Greek mathematician, physicist and astronomer. He was known as the leading scientist in classical antiquity. His mathematical work is to modern in technique that it is barely distinguishable from that of 17ᵗʰ century mathematicians. It was all done without the benefits of algebra or of a convenient number system. He also developed general method for finding the areas and volumes. He used the method to find areas banded by parabolas and spirals and to find volume of cylinders, paraboloids and segments of spheres. Archimedes also gave a procedure to find approximating values of π and banded its value [3 10/11,3 1/7]. He also invented a method to find the square roots and proposed another method based on the Greek myriad for representing numbers as large as one followed by 80 million billion zeros. Archimedes was most proud of his discovery of a method for finding the volume of a sphere. He showed that the volume of a sphere is 2/3 the volume of the cylinder. The method of mechanical theorems, which was the part of palimpsest found in the Constantinople in 1906. In that treatise Archimedes explain how he made some of his discoveries that are participating in the main idea of the integral calculus.'),figure('archimedes-source162','Archimedes (207BC–212BC)','Original source portrait and printed dates.'),note('ILATE is a useful selection guide, not a rule that guarantees the simplest integral. The printed π bounds [3 10/11,3 1/7] have the lower endpoint greater than the upper endpoint and do not bracket π; this source typo is preserved. The history caption’s dates are also printed in reversed chronological order.')])]
